-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 13 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-23
-- Topic: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L2 — Clearly trivial threshold application
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Maplewood Catering records a $75 office-supply expense in the wrong period. The auditor has set overall materiality at $200,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should classify this misstatement as:', '["Clearly trivial and not required to be accumulated", "Immaterial but required to be accumulated with other misstatements", "A factual misstatement requiring immediate correction by management", "A projected misstatement requiring extrapolation to the population"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, misstatements are clearly trivial when they are inconsequential both individually and in the aggregate. A $75 timing difference against $200,000 materiality is far below any reasonable threshold and need not be accumulated on the summary of uncorrected misstatements. Choice B is incorrect because accumulation is only required for misstatements that exceed the clearly trivial threshold, not for amounts this inconsequential relative to materiality.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Threshold distinguishing significant deficiency from control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, what elevates a control deficiency to a significant deficiency?', '["The deficiency has persisted for more than one audit period without remediation by management", "The deficiency is first identified during fieldwork rather than during the planning phase of the audit", "The deficiency is severe enough to merit the attention of those charged with governance", "The deficiency was identified by the entity''s internal audit function rather than the external auditor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.08, a significant deficiency is a deficiency, or combination of deficiencies, in internal control that is severe enough to merit the attention of those charged with governance. The classification depends on severity relative to financial reporting impact, not on when or by whom it was discovered. Choice A is incorrect because the duration of a deficiency may factor into its severity assessment but does not alone determine whether it rises to the significant deficiency threshold.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Responsibility for internal control design
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, which party is responsible for designing and maintaining internal control over financial reporting?', '["The external auditor engaged to perform the annual audit", "The entity''s internal audit function on behalf of the board", "Those charged with governance acting independently of management", "Management of the entity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.02, management is responsible for the design, implementation, and maintenance of internal control relevant to the preparation and fair presentation of financial statements. The auditor''s role is limited to identifying and communicating control deficiencies discovered during the audit. Choice A is incorrect because the external auditor evaluates internal controls as part of the audit engagement but has no responsibility for their design or ongoing maintenance.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Management representation letter regarding uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Clearwater Dynamics Inc., the engagement team accumulated six uncorrected misstatements totaling $38,000. Overall materiality is $250,000. Before issuing the audit report, the auditor requests management to sign a representation letter. Under AU-C 580, the letter should include:', '["An assertion that no additional misstatements exist beyond those already identified by the audit team", "A statement that management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial both individually and in the aggregate", "A commitment by management to correct all accumulated misstatements in the subsequent period", "An acknowledgment that the auditor has communicated every control deficiency identified during the engagement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.16, the auditor must obtain written representations from management that it believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements aggregated during the audit are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of uncorrected items is typically attached to the letter. Choice A is incorrect because the representation addresses management''s belief about the immateriality of specific uncorrected items, not a blanket guarantee that no other misstatements exist.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Communication timing for significant deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, when must the auditor communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance?', '["No later than 60 days following the report release date", "Within 30 days of discovering the deficiency during fieldwork", "Immediately upon identification during audit testing procedures", "Before the engagement letter is signed for the next audit period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.13, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses identified during the audit in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date. This deadline ensures timely communication while allowing the auditor to complete evaluations. Choice B is incorrect because the standard ties the communication deadline to the report release date, not to the date the deficiency was discovered during fieldwork.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — IT general control deficiency effect on risk assessment
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Pinnacle Retail Group, the engagement team discovers that the IT administrator can modify transaction records in the accounting system without a segregated approval process. The system processes over $12 million in transactions annually. What is the most likely effect on the auditor''s assessment?', '["The auditor should increase the acceptable level of detection risk to reduce testing costs", "The auditor should reduce the assessed level of inherent risk for IT-related transactions", "The auditor should assess a higher risk of material misstatement and expand substantive testing", "The auditor should withdraw from the engagement because the control environment is unreliable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 315.14, when IT general controls are deficient, the auditor cannot rely on automated controls that depend on those IT systems. Unrestricted administrative access without segregation of duties increases the risk of unauthorized modifications, requiring the auditor to assess higher risk and design more extensive substantive procedures. Choice A is incorrect because the auditor should reduce detection risk by performing more substantive work, not increase it, which would mean accepting a higher chance of failing to detect material misstatements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Prior-period misstatement carryforward effect
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Brookfield Energy Corp. had a $65,000 overstatement of prepaid insurance at December 31, 2024, that was not corrected. Overall materiality for the 2025 audit is $300,000. The auditor evaluating the 2025 financial statements should consider this prior-period misstatement by:', '["Ignoring the prior-period error because it was below materiality when originally identified", "Determining whether the prior-period misstatement affects the current-period financial statements through its reversing effect", "Requiring management to restate the 2024 comparative financial statements before the audit can proceed", "Treating the prior-period misstatement as a subsequent event requiring separate disclosure in the notes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.06, the auditor must evaluate the effect of uncorrected misstatements from prior periods on the current-period financial statements. A prior-period overstatement of prepaid insurance reverses into the current period as an understatement of insurance expense, potentially creating a current-period misstatement. Choice A is incorrect because prior-period misstatements do not simply disappear; their reversing effect may cause a misstatement in the current year that must be evaluated against current materiality.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Auditor accumulation requirement for identified misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what is the auditor required to do with misstatements identified during the audit that are not clearly trivial?', '["Immediately report each misstatement to the applicable regulatory authority", "Accumulate them solely for the purpose of calculating the final audit fee", "Disclose each misstatement individually in an emphasis-of-matter paragraph in the auditor''s report", "Accumulate them and communicate them to the appropriate level of management"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor must accumulate all identified misstatements during the audit, other than those that are clearly trivial, and communicate them to the appropriate level of management on a timely basis. This accumulation enables the auditor to evaluate the aggregate effect on the financial statements. Choice A is incorrect because misstatements are communicated to entity management for potential correction, not reported directly to regulators; regulatory reporting obligations arise only in specific limited circumstances.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors in misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Trident Marine Services, the auditor identifies a $28,000 uncorrected misstatement from a related-party transaction that was not properly disclosed. Overall materiality is $500,000. The misstatement is quantitatively immaterial. What is the most likely effect of qualitative factors on the auditor''s evaluation?', '["The auditor must waive the misstatement because it falls below the quantitative materiality threshold", "The misstatement has no bearing on the opinion because related-party disclosures are considered supplementary information", "The auditor may conclude the misstatement is material due to its nature despite being below quantitative thresholds", "The auditor should reduce overall materiality to a level that captures all related-party transactions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A16, the auditor must consider both quantitative and qualitative factors when evaluating misstatements. Related-party transactions carry heightened qualitative significance because they may indicate management bias or undisclosed relationships, potentially making a quantitatively immaterial misstatement qualitatively material. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 450 explicitly requires consideration of qualitative characteristics alongside quantitative amounts; the auditor cannot dismiss a misstatement solely because its dollar amount falls below the numerical threshold.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Control deviation rate and deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Redstone Logistics LLC, the auditor tests a sample of 40 purchase order approvals and finds that 6 lacked the required manager signature. The control is designed to prevent unauthorized purchases exceeding $5,000. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should evaluate this control deficiency by considering:', '["The rate of deviation relative to the expected rate and the magnitude of transactions processed without the control operating", "Only the dollar amount of the six unapproved purchase orders compared to overall materiality", "Whether the entity has purchased fidelity bond insurance to cover any unauthorized transactions", "The length of time the specific manager was unavailable to review and sign the purchase orders"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A8, evaluating control deficiency severity requires considering both the likelihood that the deficiency could result in a misstatement and the magnitude of the potential misstatement. A 15% deviation rate significantly exceeds typical expected rates, indicating the control is not operating reliably across the population of transactions it protects. Choice B is incorrect because the evaluation must consider the deviation rate and total volume of transactions at risk, not just the dollar impact of the observed sample deviations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Preventive vs detective control interaction
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Whitfield Consumer Products has a preventive control requiring dual authorization for journal entries above $10,000 and a detective control where the controller reviews a monthly report of all posted entries. The auditor discovers the dual-authorization control has not been enforced for six months while the monthly review has operated consistently. What is the most likely effect on the auditor''s deficiency evaluation?', '["The detective control fully compensates for the preventive control failure, so no deficiency exists", "The auditor should evaluate whether the detective control sufficiently reduces the severity of the preventive control deficiency", "The auditor must classify both controls as material weaknesses because they address the same financial reporting assertion", "The preventive control failure is irrelevant because detective controls are considered more reliable under auditing standards"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A11, the auditor considers compensating controls when evaluating deficiency severity. A functioning detective control may reduce but does not automatically eliminate the severity of a failed preventive control. The auditor must assess whether the detective control operates at sufficient precision to catch misstatements the preventive control would have prevented. Choice A is incorrect because compensating controls must be specifically evaluated for effectiveness; automatic full compensation is never assumed under the standard.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Projected misstatement exceeds tolerable misstatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Cascade Mountain Brewing, the auditor samples 30 inventory counts from a population of 1,500 items. The sample reveals pricing errors totaling $11,500. The tolerable misstatement for inventory is $45,000, and the projected misstatement to the population is $575,000. Under AU-C 530, the auditor should:', '["Accept the population because the sample errors of $11,500 fall below the $45,000 tolerable misstatement", "Request management to recount only the 30 sampled items and correct the identified pricing errors", "Conclude that the projected misstatement exceeds tolerable misstatement and perform additional procedures or request adjustment", "Reduce the tolerable misstatement to $11,500 to align with actual sample results"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 530.13, when the projected misstatement exceeds tolerable misstatement, the sampling results do not support the recorded population amount. With a $575,000 projected misstatement far exceeding the $45,000 tolerable threshold, the auditor must expand testing, request management to investigate and adjust, or consider the impact on the audit opinion. Choice A is incorrect because the relevant comparison is the projected misstatement against tolerable misstatement, not the raw sample errors against the tolerable amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Uncorrected misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what is an uncorrected misstatement?', '["A misstatement the auditor has accumulated during the audit that has not been corrected by management", "A misstatement discovered after the report release date that requires a formal restatement", "A misstatement that affects only the notes to the financial statements and not any account balances", "A misstatement that falls below the clearly trivial threshold established during audit planning"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.06, uncorrected misstatements are those the auditor has accumulated during the current audit and from prior periods that have not been corrected by management. The auditor evaluates these misstatements individually and in the aggregate to determine their effect on the financial statements. Choice D is incorrect because misstatements below the clearly trivial threshold are excluded from accumulation entirely and are therefore not classified as uncorrected misstatements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluate opinion modification for pervasive misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pacific Coast Distributing, the engagement team identifies uncorrected misstatements affecting revenue, inventory, and accounts receivable totaling $420,000. Overall materiality is $350,000. The misstatements affect multiple financial statement line items and involve different transaction classes. Management declines to make corrections. The engagement partner must evaluate whether to issue:', '["An unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph explaining the misstatements", "A disclaimer of opinion because management''s refusal limits the scope of the audit", "A qualified opinion because the misstatements are material but confined to specific accounts", "An adverse opinion because the misstatements are both material and pervasive across the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 705.09, when uncorrected misstatements are both material and pervasive to the financial statements, the auditor must issue an adverse opinion. Here, the $420,000 aggregate exceeds $350,000 materiality, and the misstatements span revenue, inventory, and receivables—affecting multiple elements and transaction classes—meeting the pervasiveness threshold. Choice C is incorrect because a qualified opinion is appropriate only when misstatements are material but not pervasive; the breadth of affected line items here indicates pervasive impact requiring an adverse opinion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Auditor not required to search for deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Sterling Capital Partners, a junior staff member asks whether the firm should have designed specific audit procedures to search for control deficiencies. Under AU-C 265, the engagement partner should explain that the auditor:', '["Must perform specific procedures designed to identify every deficiency in internal control", "Is not required to perform procedures specifically to identify deficiencies but should communicate those identified during the audit", "Should search for deficiencies only when control risk is assessed at the maximum level", "Must engage a specialist to perform a separate evaluation of internal control effectiveness"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.04, the auditor is not required to design and perform procedures solely to identify deficiencies in internal control during a financial statement audit. However, AU-C 265.09 requires the auditor to evaluate any deficiencies identified during the audit and communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses appropriately. Choice A is incorrect because a financial statement audit does not impose a duty to search for all control deficiencies; the auditor communicates those identified as a byproduct of performing normal audit procedures.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: hard / L3 — Iron curtain vs rollover approach for cumulative misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Horizon Aerospace Inc. has carried forward uncorrected misstatements from three prior years totaling $180,000. Current-year misstatements add $40,000. Overall materiality is $250,000. Using the rollover approach, aggregate current-year misstatements are $40,000. Using the iron curtain approach, the cumulative balance sheet effect is $220,000. What is the most likely effect on the auditor''s evaluation?', '["The auditor should consider both approaches and use the one producing the more conservative result when assessing the aggregate effect on the financial statements", "The auditor should use only the rollover approach because prior-year misstatements were already evaluated in previous audits", "The auditor should use only the iron curtain approach because cumulative balance sheet effects are always more relevant than current-year amounts", "The auditor should average the two approaches to arrive at a blended misstatement figure of $130,000 for evaluation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A7 and SEC Staff Accounting Bulletin No. 108, auditors should consider both the rollover approach (current-year effect on the income statement) and the iron curtain approach (cumulative effect on the balance sheet) when evaluating misstatements. Relying solely on one method could allow material misstatements to accumulate undetected. Choice B is incorrect because the rollover approach alone may permit material balance sheet misstatements to build over time without triggering correction, which is precisely the concern the dual-approach framework addresses.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Fraud-related control deficiency classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Birchwood Financial Group, the engagement team discovers that the CFO manually overrode an automated revenue cutoff control to accelerate recognition of $95,000 in fees before quarter-end. The override was concealed by backdating journal entries. Materiality is $400,000. The auditor should evaluate this deficiency as:', '["A deficiency in design because the automated control lacked a lockout feature for senior management", "A significant deficiency because the $95,000 amount is below overall materiality", "An inconsequential control weakness that requires only oral communication to management", "At least a significant deficiency and likely a material weakness because it involves intentional management override indicative of fraud"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, a control deficiency involving fraud by senior management is presumed to be at least a significant deficiency and should be evaluated as a potential material weakness regardless of the dollar amount. Intentional management override with concealment indicates a breakdown in the control environment that undermines the reliability of financial reporting broadly. Choice B is incorrect because the quantitative comparison to materiality does not control the classification when fraud by management is involved; the qualitative severity of intentional override elevates the deficiency independently of the dollar threshold.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Recurring misstatements indicate control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Summit Ridge Manufacturing has reported inventory valuation misstatements in each of the past three audits. Each year the misstatements were individually below materiality and management declined to correct them. The auditor for the current year should analyze the recurrence by considering whether:', '["The prior-year misstatements automatically expire after three years and no longer require accumulation", "The recurring nature of the misstatements indicates a control deficiency that may rise to the level of a significant deficiency or material weakness", "Each year''s misstatements are evaluated independently and prior-year patterns are irrelevant to the current assessment", "The auditor should reduce materiality each year until the recurring misstatements are captured"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A9, the auditor considers the history of misstatements when evaluating control deficiency severity. Recurring misstatements of the same type across multiple periods strongly suggest that the underlying control is either missing or not operating effectively, potentially elevating the deficiency to a significant deficiency or material weakness. Choice C is incorrect because AU-C 450.06 requires the auditor to consider prior-period uncorrected misstatements and their patterns, not to evaluate each year in isolation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Documentation requirements for control deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, what must the auditor document regarding identified control deficiencies?', '["The basis for the auditor''s determination of whether each identified deficiency is a significant deficiency or material weakness", "Only those deficiencies that management has agreed to remediate before the next audit", "A complete redesign of the entity''s internal control system addressing each identified weakness", "The estimated dollar amount that each control deficiency will cost the entity to remediate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.11, the auditor must document the basis for the determination of whether identified control deficiencies, individually or in combination, are significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. This documentation supports the auditor''s evaluation and the resulting communications. Choice B is incorrect because the documentation requirement applies to all identified deficiencies evaluated by the auditor, not only those management has agreed to fix.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Subsequent discovery of misstatement after report release
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After releasing the audit report for Eastport Shipping Co., the auditor learns that a $310,000 inventory write-down was omitted from the December 31, 2025, financial statements. Overall materiality was $275,000. Under AU-C 560, the auditor should:', '["Take no action because the audit has been completed and the report has already been released", "Notify the SEC directly without consulting management or those charged with governance", "Discuss the matter with management, determine whether the financial statements need revision, and consider the effect on the auditor''s report", "Issue a new engagement letter for a separate engagement to investigate the omission"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 560.14, when the auditor becomes aware of facts after the report release date that might have caused the auditor to revise the report, the auditor must discuss the matter with management and determine whether revision is needed. A $310,000 omission against $275,000 materiality is material and would likely require restatement and a revised auditor''s report. Choice A is incorrect because the auditor''s responsibilities do not end at report release; AU-C 560 imposes an obligation to act when subsequently discovered facts indicate the financial statements may be materially misstated.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Known misstatement from specific item testing
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what distinguishes a known misstatement from a projected misstatement?', '["A known misstatement is identified only through analytical procedures while a projected misstatement comes from detailed testing", "A known misstatement is always material while a projected misstatement is always immaterial", "A known misstatement requires management to restate prior periods while a projected misstatement does not", "A known misstatement is identified with certainty in specific items while a projected misstatement is estimated from sample results"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A3, a known misstatement is one about which there is no doubt—it has been specifically identified in a transaction, balance, or disclosure. A projected misstatement, by contrast, is the auditor''s best estimate of misstatement in a population based on extrapolating errors found in a sample. Choice A is incorrect because known misstatements typically arise from detailed testing of specific items, not analytical procedures, and projected misstatements arise from sampling, not detailed testing of every item.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Remediated deficiency still requires communication
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Lakewood Regional Hospital, the engagement team identifies a material weakness in revenue cycle controls in October 2025. Management remediates the deficiency and implements a new approval workflow by November 15, 2025. The auditor tests the remediated control through December 31, 2025, and confirms it is operating effectively. The engagement partner should evaluate whether to:', '["Omit the material weakness from the written communication because it was fully remediated before year-end", "Communicate the material weakness in writing to those charged with governance despite its remediation because it existed during the period under audit", "Downgrade the finding to a deficiency in operation and exclude it from the formal communication", "Delay communication until the next audit to confirm the remediated control continues to operate effectively"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A19, the auditor should communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses identified during the audit even if they have been remediated by period-end. The deficiency existed during the period under audit and may have affected financial reporting before remediation. Those charged with governance need this information for oversight purposes. Choice A is incorrect because remediation does not eliminate the communication requirement; the deficiency''s existence during the audit period is what triggers the obligation to communicate under AU-C 265.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Weak control environment increases severity of other deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Crestline Development Corp., the auditor identifies several minor control deficiencies in expense reimbursement processing. The auditor also observes that the CEO routinely overrides established policies and that the board does not actively oversee financial reporting. How does the weak control environment most likely affect the auditor''s evaluation of the individual deficiencies?', '["The weak control environment increases the severity of the individual deficiencies because pervasive entity-level weaknesses reduce the effectiveness of other controls", "The control environment is evaluated separately and has no bearing on the classification of individual processing deficiencies", "The auditor should report the control environment weakness but reclassify each processing deficiency as immaterial", "The individual deficiencies should be evaluated solely on their own merits regardless of the entity-level control environment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A10, the control environment influences the effectiveness of all other components of internal control. When the control environment is weak—such as when management overrides policies and governance oversight is lacking—the severity of individual process-level deficiencies increases because entity-level compensating controls cannot be relied upon to mitigate them. Choice B is incorrect because AU-C 315 requires the auditor to evaluate controls holistically; a weak control environment directly undermines the reliability of process-level controls.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Evaluate competing positions on misstatement classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Aspen Valley Hospitality recorded a $145,000 allowance for doubtful accounts based on management''s judgment that recent economic conditions will reduce collection rates. The auditor''s independent estimate, using historical data and current aging analysis, suggests the allowance should be $210,000. Management contends its estimate is reasonable because the model incorporates forward-looking assumptions. Materiality is $100,000. The engagement partner should evaluate this situation by:', '["Accepting management''s estimate without further analysis because estimates inherently involve judgment", "Determining whether the $65,000 difference represents a judgmental misstatement and evaluating it against materiality together with other accumulated misstatements", "Requiring management to adopt the auditor''s estimate of $210,000 as the only acceptable amount", "Issuing a disclaimer of opinion because the disagreement over the estimate creates a scope limitation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A4, when the auditor concludes that management''s estimate is unreasonable or when the difference between the auditor''s estimate and management''s recorded amount is a misstatement, the difference is classified as a judgmental misstatement. The $65,000 difference must be added to other accumulated misstatements and evaluated against materiality. Choice C is incorrect because the auditor cannot require management to adopt the auditor''s point estimate; rather, the auditor evaluates whether management''s estimate falls within a reasonable range and accumulates any difference as a misstatement.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Written representation about management responsibility for IC
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Granite Peak Holdings requests a management representation letter before issuing the audit report. Under AU-C 580, which representation regarding internal control should management provide?', '["That all significant control deficiencies have been remediated before the balance sheet date", "That the entity''s controls have been independently tested and confirmed effective by internal audit", "That management acknowledges its responsibility for designing, implementing, and maintaining internal control relevant to financial reporting", "That internal controls are sufficient to prevent all material misstatements in the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.10(b), the auditor must obtain a written representation from management acknowledging responsibility for the design, implementation, and maintenance of internal control relevant to the preparation and fair presentation of financial statements free from material misstatement. This confirms the foundational allocation of responsibility. Choice D is incorrect because no system of internal control can prevent all material misstatements; the representation addresses management''s responsibility for the system, not a guarantee of its perfection.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Multiple interacting deficiencies across IT and manual controls
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Silverton National Bank, the engagement team identifies three deficiencies: (1) the user access review for the core banking system was not performed for eight months, (2) the manual account reconciliation process lacks an independent review step, and (3) exception reports generated by the system are not investigated timely. Individually, each deficiency is classified as a significant deficiency. The engagement partner should evaluate whether:', '["Each deficiency should be communicated separately to management without further combined assessment", "The three deficiencies should be averaged to determine an overall severity rating for the bank''s control environment", "The combination of deficiencies constitutes a material weakness because together they create a reasonable possibility of an undetected material misstatement", "Only the IT access deficiency requires further evaluation since the manual deficiencies are compensated by the automated system"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, the auditor must evaluate whether individually insignificant or significant deficiencies combine to create a material weakness. Here, the IT access gap means unauthorized transactions could enter the system, the lack of reconciliation review means they might not be caught, and uninvestigated exception reports mean anomalies go unresolved. Together, these create a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement could occur and not be detected. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 explicitly requires the auditor to consider the combined effect of deficiencies, not merely communicate each one in isolation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Misstatements in disclosures treated same as balances
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Thornton Pharmaceuticals, the auditor discovers that a required disclosure about contingent litigation was omitted from the notes. The potential exposure is $190,000, and overall materiality is $250,000. Management argues the omission is not a misstatement because it does not affect any account balance. How should the auditor analyze management''s position?', '["The omission of a required disclosure is a misstatement under AU-C 450 regardless of whether it affects account balances, and must be evaluated against materiality", "Management is correct that only account balance errors qualify as misstatements under auditing standards", "The auditor should accept management''s position because disclosure misstatements do not affect the audit opinion", "The omission is relevant only if the litigation is resolved before the report release date"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.04, a misstatement includes differences in disclosures compared to what is required by the applicable financial reporting framework. The omission of a required contingent litigation disclosure is a misstatement that the auditor must accumulate and evaluate for materiality, just as with account balance errors. Choice B is incorrect because AU-C 450 defines misstatements broadly to encompass amounts, classifications, presentation, and disclosures—not just account balances.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Classification misstatement between line items
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Copperfield Industries recorded $85,000 of capital lease payments entirely as operating lease expense instead of splitting the amount between interest expense and lease liability reduction. Overall materiality is $300,000. Under AU-C 450, this error is best classified as:', '["A disclosure misstatement because it affects only the notes to the financial statements", "An offsetting misstatement that has no net effect on the financial statements", "A classification misstatement because revenue and expense line items are misstated even though total expenses are unchanged", "A clearly trivial error because the net income effect is zero"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A1, a misstatement includes errors in classification, which occur when amounts are reported in incorrect financial statement line items. Although the total cash outflow is the same, misclassifying capital lease payments as operating expense misstates both the interest expense line and the lease liability balance, affecting the presentation of the financial statements. Choice B is incorrect because the misstatement does not truly offset—different line items on both the income statement and balance sheet are misstated, which affects the usefulness and accuracy of the financial statement presentation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Performance materiality purpose
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 320, what is the primary purpose of setting performance materiality below overall materiality?', '["To establish a separate threshold for communicating control deficiencies to those charged with governance", "To create a buffer that ensures the total of uncorrected and undetected misstatements is unlikely to exceed overall materiality", "To define the dollar threshold at which the auditor must withdraw from the engagement", "To determine the maximum amount of sampling error permitted in any single substantive test"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 320.09, performance materiality is set below overall materiality to reduce to an appropriately low level the probability that the aggregate of uncorrected and undetected misstatements exceeds overall materiality. It serves as a planning buffer that accounts for the possibility of undetected errors. Choice A is incorrect because performance materiality relates to the scope and nature of audit procedures, not to the threshold for communicating control deficiencies, which is governed by AU-C 265.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Sampling risk impact on projected misstatement reliability
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Fieldstone Construction Group, the auditor projects a $48,000 misstatement to a $6 million accounts receivable population based on a sample of 40 items. The tolerable misstatement is $60,000. A second auditor notes that the $12,000 cushion between the projected and tolerable amounts may not be sufficient given sampling risk. What is the most likely analytical implication?', '["The projected misstatement is below tolerable misstatement, so the population is automatically accepted regardless of sampling risk", "Sampling risk means the true population misstatement could exceed the projected amount, and the narrow cushion increases the risk that the actual misstatement exceeds tolerable misstatement", "Sampling risk only applies to statistical sampling and is irrelevant when the auditor uses nonstatistical methods", "The auditor should ignore sampling risk and rely solely on the point estimate of the projected misstatement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 530.A20, sampling risk is the possibility that the auditor''s conclusion based on a sample differs from what would result from testing the entire population. When the projected misstatement is close to tolerable misstatement, as here with only a $12,000 cushion, sampling risk increases the probability that the true population misstatement actually exceeds the tolerable threshold. Choice A is incorrect because a projected misstatement below the tolerable amount does not guarantee acceptance; the auditor must consider sampling risk and whether the proximity of the two amounts provides sufficient assurance.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
